[38;2;124;124;124mWe have X = {1, 2, ..., 20}, P = set of all 9-element subsets of X. For any map [0m[38;2;124;124;124mf: P → X, we need to find a 10-element subset Y of X such that f(Y \ {k}) ≠ k [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfor any k in Y.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo for each 9-element subset S of X, f(S) is some element of X. We need Y (10-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124melement subset) such that for every k ∈ Y, f(Y \ {k}) ≠ k.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNote: Y \ {k} is a 9-element subset, so f(Y \ {k}) is defined. The condition is [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthat f(Y \ {k}) ≠ k for all k ∈ Y.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a classic combinatorics problem. Let me think about the approach.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis resembles the "Kneser graph" or "Katona" type problems, or perhaps a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mproblem solvable by the probabilistic method or a counting argument.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about it differently. For each 9-element subset S, f(S) is some [0m
